-- title:
--   Recognition of one-variable function fields via valuations
-- statement:
--   Let $K \subseteq F$ be fields, with $F$ a $K$-algebra via $\mathrm{algebraMap}$, and let $V$ be a set of valuations on $F$ with values in $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$, the integers written multiplicatively with a zero adjoined, so that $v(g) > 1$ expresses that $g$ has a pole at $v$ and $v(g) \le 1$ that $g$ is integral at $v$. Assume: (i) every $v \in V$ is trivial on the image of $K$, that is $v(c) = 1$ for every nonzero $c \in K$; (ii) every $v \in V$ has residue field generated by the constants in the sense that whenever $g \in F$ satisfies $v(g) = 1$ there is $c \in K$ with $v(g - c) < 1$; (iii) every nonzero $g \in F$ has $1 < v(g)$ for only finitely many $v \in V$; (iv) any $g \in F$ with $v(g) \le 1$ for all $v \in V$ lies in the image of $K$. Then for every $f \in F$ outside the image of $K$, the element $f$ is transcendental over $K$ and $F$ is a finite-dimensional vector space over the intermediate field $K(f)$ obtained by adjoining $f$ to $K$ inside $F$.
--
--   This is the classical valuation-theoretic criterion recognising $F$ as an algebraic function field of one variable over $K$: conditions (i)–(iv) say that $V$ behaves like the set of points of a complete curve with constant field $K$, and the conclusion gives transcendence degree one together with finiteness over $K(f)$ for any non-constant $f$. It is applied to the field of invariant meromorphic functions in the Čerednik–Drinfeld setting, where a quotient of the rigid upper half plane by a discrete cocompact group must be identified with an algebraic curve.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_transcendental_and_finiteDimensional_adjoin_of_valuations.lean

